[158.948076, "o", "- No software CVE patch is required for initial entry remediation. Technical remediation must focus on hardening PostgreSQL authentication configuration (`pg_hba.conf`) and restricting database superuser program execution privileges.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[158.948142, "o", "> EOF\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[158.948873, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@654d0bd251de:/app# "]
[174.377666, "i", "cat <<'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline Configuration Assessment Report\n\n## Overview\nThis report details configuration weaknesses, policy compliance failures, and baseline security deviations identified on host `postgres-prod-01` during forensic investigation.\n\n## Identified Baseline Failures\n\n### 1. PostgreSQL Permissive Host Authentication (`pg_hba.conf`)\n- **Check Reference**: `CHK-06` / `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`, `/sas/alerts.json:17`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` (`pg_hba.conf grants 'trust' auth for 0.0.0.0/0`).\n- **Risk Impact**: Configuring `trust` authentication for network wildcard address `0.0.0.0/0` allows any TCP/IP client to connect as any PostgreSQL user (including superuser `postgres`) without providing a password. This completely bypasses authentication controls.\n\n### 2. Superuser Password & Privilege Controls\n- **Reference**: CTI Advisory Step 1 (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`, `T1078`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` and PostgreSQL database default privileges.\n- **Risk Impact**: Weak database superuser controls combined with `trust` auth allowed remote attackers to execute `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` statements without authorization, granting direct system command execution as user `postgres`.\n\n### 3. Unrestricted Egress & DNS Traffic Controls\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3` (DNS tunnel configuration targeting resolver `update-postgres.example.cn`).\n- **Risk Impact**: Absence of outbound egress filtering and internal DNS query monitoring allowed covert C2 communication and data exfiltration over arbitrary DNS queries.\n\n### 4. Cron & Systemd Service Modification Monitoring Gaps\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5` and `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`.\n- **Risk Impact**: Lack of file integrity monitoring (FIM) or auditing on systemd unit directories (`/etc/systemd/system/`) and spool directories (`/var/spool/cron/`) allowed silent persistence to remain undetected by automated security product feeds.\n\n## Recommended Baseline Configuration Standard\n1. Modify `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` to replace `trust` with `scram-sha-256` or `md5` authentication, and restrict client address ranges to specific trusted subnets.\n2. Enforce strong password requirements for database superuser accounts.\n3. Implement file integrity monitoring (FIM) on system cron directories and systemd unit paths.\n4. Restrict outbound DNS traffic to designated internal recursive resolvers.\nEOF\n"]
